   "title": "Inject Video Latent",
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "WHY THIS NODE EXISTS\n\nH3's stock nodes ALWAYS build a zeros latent. References are conditioning that is re-injected every step - never a starting point. So there is no video-to-video path out of the box.\n\nThis encodes the real cropped frames into the VIDEO stream of the joint AV latent, leaving the audio stream alone. That turns SamplerCustomAdvanced + denoise<1.0 into ordinary img2img.\n\nFrame count must sit on H3's 17k+5 grid (5, 22, 39 ... 175, 226, 362). Clips generated by H3 already do."
   ],
   "color": "#432",
   "bgcolor": "#653"
  },
  {
   "id": 101,
   "type": "Note",
   "pos": [
    -705,
    850
   ],
   "size": [
    360,
    285
   ],
   "flags": {},
   "order": 8,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "title": "Why per-frame crop",
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "PER-FRAME CROPPING IS MANDATORY\n\nA single fixed crop is sized by the widest frame. On a push-in the face is then STILL SMALL inside that crop - on exactly the frames that needed help. The benefit vanishes where it matters.\n\nSo the crop SIZE varies per frame, holding the face at a constant fraction of every crop (crop_factor). Every crop is then resized to ONE canvas, because H3 generates a single fixed WxH for a whole sequence.\n\nResult: the face is always large in H3's input, however small it was in the source.\n\nSMOOTHING\nsize_smooth_window (51) is deliberately larger than smooth_window (21). Size jitter makes the crop breathe, which changes the resample factor every frame and reads as shimmer.\n\ngaussian beats savgol here - polynomial fits preserve sharp features by design, which is the opposite of what you want."
   ],
   "color": "#432",
   "bgcolor": "#653"
  },
